---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
const submit = "https://github.com/devstreamhq/devstream/issues/new?template=submit-repo.yml";
---

<Base title="How DevStream works" description="How repos get listed, how Likes are counted, and why some repos leave the list.">
  <div class="wrap page">
    <h1>How it works</h1>
    <p>Short version: you submit a repo, a bot checks it, developers Like it, and the list sorts itself. Here's the slightly longer one.</p>

    <h2>Getting listed</h2>
    <p>
      Open a <a href={submit}>submit issue</a> with the repo, a category and a line on what it does. A bot checks it against the quality bar:
      an open source licence, a README with real content, a description, a push in the last 90 days and an age of at least 30 days. Pass, and
      it's on the list. You can submit three repos a week.
    </p>

    <h2>Counting Likes</h2>
    <p>
      Every listed repo gets a Discussion. A thumbs-up there is a Like, as long as it comes from an account that's at least 30 days old with a
      public repo or a contribution in the last year. Anyone newer doesn't count at all. And if a pile of Likes lands at once from accounts
      only a few months old, the repo is flagged and kept out of Trending until someone checks it.
    </p>

    <h2>Leaving the list</h2>
    <p>
      Repos that stop getting pushes for a year, get archived or call themselves unmaintained get a 30-day warning, then move to the <a
        href="/archived">archive</a>. Repos that disappear or drop their licence or README get a week's notice, then come off.
    </p>

    <h2>The full docs</h2>
    <p>The quality bar, the trust rule and how reports work are all in the <a href="https://github.com/devstreamhq/devstream/wiki">wiki</a>.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
